# System User Authorization File
# Format: USERNAME|PASSWORD_HASH|UIC_GROUP|UIC_MEMBER|DEFAULT_DIR|FLAGS|PRIVILEGES
#
# Password hash is SHA256 hex. An EMPTY hash means the account CANNOT
# AUTHENTICATE -- no password, right or wrong, is accepted (vms-08f).
#
# THIS WAS NOT ALWAYS TRUE. Every account here originally shipped with an
# empty hash, and sysuaf_authenticate() (src/libvms/rtl/sysuaf.c) treated
# an empty hash as "no password required" -- an auth bypass, not a
# feature. vms-72c measured it directly against a real QEMU boot ('SYSTEM'
# + a deliberately wrong password reached a DCL prompt) and closed it for
# the two accounts its own UAT drove: SYSTEM (real hash below) and GUEST
# (real hash below). vms-08f is the follow-up that found the SAME bypass
# still live for the other four rows -- OPERATOR, DEFAULT, USER1, USER2 --
# because the first fix scoped itself to the accounts a test happened to
# exercise rather than to the rule an empty hash obeys everywhere.
#
# THE FIX IS IN THE RULE, NOT IN THIS DATA. sysuaf_authenticate() no
# longer special-cases an empty hash as a green light; every account row
# below, present or future, is bound by the same refusal. Per Rule 10
# (OVMX CLAUDE.md), the disposition is pinned to public OpenVMS
# documentation, not self-certified:
#   - OpenVMS Guide to System Security, S7.3.1 "Types of Passwords":
#     "With the exception of an automatic login account, all users must
#     have at least one password to log in."
#   - The one documented passwordless state is UAI$M_AUTOLOGIN, an
#     EXPLICIT per-account flag scoped to a bound terminal/batch/network
#     path -- not the default meaning of an absent password field, and
#     not implemented by OVMX. See the disposition comment on
#     sysuaf_authenticate() in src/libvms/rtl/sysuaf.c for the full pin.
#
# CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS FILE: OPERATOR/DEFAULT/USER1/USER2 below still
# carry no hash. That used to mean "logs in as anyone"; it now means
# "cannot log in interactively at all" until an operator assigns each a
# real password via AUTHORIZE -- a deliberate, visible lockout, which is
# the safe failure mode Rule 10 requires, not a silent hole. Assigning
# those four accounts real passwords is unstarted follow-up work this
# item does not scope (see rd vms-08f); this file only had to stop being
# an open door, and per the rule change above it now cannot be one again
# by omission -- a new row added here with no hash is refused the same
# way, not silently trusted.
SYSTEM|36a708df24b4751520ee64bba2d92167294acbb8f8fbfc3a120fb75323e9739b|1|4|SYS$SYSDEVICE:[SYSMGR]||ALL
OPERATOR||1|6|SYS$SYSDEVICE:[SYSMGR]||OPER,SYSPRV,TMPMBX,NETMBX
DEFAULT||200|200|SYS$SYSDEVICE:[USERS.DEFAULT]||TMPMBX,NETMBX
GUEST|16ceb2796ccd9d52d4f2a92134ef9ecfeb8f016150a82d36b299d09d5b9963f0|200|201|SYS$SYSDEVICE:[USERS.GUEST]||TMPMBX
USER1||200|202|SYS$SYSDEVICE:[USERS.USER1]||TMPMBX,NETMBX
USER2||200|203|SYS$SYSDEVICE:[USERS.USER2]||TMPMBX,NETMBX
